WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:12.680
It took just one week. One week was really
all the time that was needed for this man,

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:18.630
the President of the United States, to have
his first diplomatic crisis. So who was it

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:26.380
with? … Well you probably know the answer
to that question: It was with Mexico.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:31.939
Donald Trump is… let’s say… “unique;”
he might be the only head of state capable

00:00:31.939 --> 00:00:36.790
of defying a foreign president in public,
Peña Nieto in this case, by telling him on

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.990
Twitter that if he doesn't travel to Washington
with the idea of paying for the wall, he may

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:42.890
as well stay at home.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:50.500
Needless to say, the visit never happened…

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.429
But don’t worry viewers, we’ve had enough
Trump talk too, the Donald is not going to

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:59.039
be the main character in this video. Today
we are going to focus on the NAFTA agreement

00:00:59.039 --> 00:01:08.220
and, especially, on the relations between
Mexico and the USA.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.990
NAFTA is a free trade agreement between the
US, Canada and Mexico. It was negotiated by

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:18.979
President George H. W. Bush in 1992 and it
was ratified and implemented by Bill Clinton

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:29.890
in 1994. This means NAFTA has been supported
both by Democrats and Republicans.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:35.619
But let's take a look back and analyse which
events took place south of the Bravo River…

00:01:35.619 --> 00:01:41.970
Let’s travel back in time to the1980s, a
time when Mexico was exhausted. The country’s

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:46.640
economic model was a worn out closed economy
and they were in state of crisis.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.579
In fact, it was at that time that massive
emigration from Mexico to the US started to

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:56.520
happen. And it wasn't just any migratory wave…
it was one of the biggest migration waves

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:09.440
of all time.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.760
And this happened in a time when American’s
weren't particularly liked in Mexico.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.700
Since the war with the US from 1846-1848 when
Mexico lost more than half of its territory,

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.410
the popular sentiment in the country was the
the US is a great villain, the great enemy

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.120
that Mexico was facing.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:31.090
By 1988 it was clear that Mexico’s closed
system was worn out and the President of Mexico,

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:35.959
who at that time was Carlos Salinas (a Harvard
graduate, by the way), decided to end this

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:38.510
and opted for opening Mexico to the world.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:40.120
In his own words:

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.010
“The US is not the worst threat, but the
best opportunity"

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:48.959
And the leaders in North America decided not
to waste this chance. They got down to business

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:54.080
and decided to bet on extending to Mexico
a free trade agreement they had recently signed

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.050
with Canada. With this, NAFTA was born.

00:02:57.050 --> 00:03:01.430
Since then, relations between the US and Mexico
have expanded a great deal.It is estimated

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:05.890
that trade between the two countries today
exceed half-a-trillion dollars.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:11.400
Now, half-a-trillion (500 billion) dollars
is quite a hard number to get your head around.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:16.640
In more relatable terms: each minute, more
than a million dollars-worth of products crosses

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:27.970
the borders of the US and Mexico.
Or, to put it in an international context,

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:32.459
trade between the US and Mexico is the equivalent
of the Chilean economy and is more than the

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:35.000
entire economy of Argentina.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.840
The current American president, Donald Trump,
threatens this agreement, and has even considered

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.840
putting an end to it because, according to
him, it is the worst agreement that the US

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.620
has ever signed.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:54.830
Now, that’s just what he says, the reality
is rather different. NAFTA has been, in general,

00:03:54.830 --> 00:04:06.379
a gold-mine for the United States.
It has been said that whenever Trump has a

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:10.730
nightmare, it’s always the same thing: he
sees a Mexican stealing his wallet.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:18.360
Anyway, I don't know if it is true (probably
not), but it would explain his obsession with

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:23.550
Mexico and also why whenever he thinks of
NAFTA he thinks of the American people getting

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:24.550
robbed.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:29.270
But, don’t think that it is only the President
who has this opinion. Many defenders of protectionism

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:34.700
in the US state that Mexican and Canadian
workers are causing American jobs to disappear

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:36.410
because of free trade.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:42.090
Specifically they claim that imports exceed
exports by almost $80 billion, and that’s

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:43.090
saying a lot!

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:47.750
But the reality is that that figures are about
as reliable as a chocolate teapot.

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:52.660
First, there are two goods that twist those
numbers which have nothing to do with NAFTA:

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:54.790
petrol and natural gas!

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:58.851
And this is really important because if we
don't consider these products, that trade

00:04:58.851 --> 00:05:02.500
hole Trump is complaining pretty much disappears.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:11.980
Alright, so maybe some of you are now wondering:
"hey, but Simon: Why wouldn't you consider

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.860
petrol and natural gas?" That’s a good question.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:18.940
Well, that’s because unless Trump wants
to take the United States back to the Stone

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:22.870
Age, he will have to keep getting all the
petrol the country needs, whether that’s

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:26.440
from Mexico, Canada, Saudi Arabia, or anywhere
else…

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.500
But these fudged figures aren't the only ones,
the protectionist dreamers also fudge the

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:36.190
figures by not considering services businesses
– this includes things like the financial

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:42.530
sector, insurance, travelling, consulting,
the internet etc etc. But ignoring these is

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:47.210
like saying banks don’t make money and Google
doesn’t exist...

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:51.320
So, if we adjust these two fudged figures,
it turns out that the USA's result is not

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:56.400
a deficit but actually a surplus (that’s
for both Mexico as well as Canada). It’s

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:05.240
actually a surplus of more than $40 billion.
In any case: the American’s are really the

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:08.160
ones who are making off like bandits in this
trade relationship.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:12.150
But alright, in Mexico it is a bit different
because, even correcting the numbers, the

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:17.810
US still gets the deficit in this relationship.
So the question is: what is the actual number?

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:23.580
Well, wait for it, approximately... drum-roll-please
[drum roll sound effect] … 0.25% of its

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:24.580
GDP.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:30.340
Yes, It’s really not much… sorry to let
you down. In fact, the deficit is smaller

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:39.950
than the one they have with China, Japan,
or Germany.

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:44.600
Almost all American industries export to Mexico
more than they import, with the exception

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:45.600
of automobiles.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:46.600
But let´s remember that most of the "Made
in Mexico" cars sold in the USA are from companies

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:52.660
of American origin, like Ford or General Motors.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:58.810
So you can guess where the profits are going
to end up…

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:02.770
But there’s more: It is said that the main
reason for the companies moving their factories

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:07.880
to Mexico is because the wages are lower,
but that is only half true, and it certainly

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:08.880
isn’t the main reason.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.290
What’s interesting about Mexico for car
companies is that Mexico has many more free

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:17.520
trade agreements with other countries than
the US, and this allows them to produce and

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.930
sell their cars in other markets like Brazil
or the European Union thus saving a lot of

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:28.080
money in tariffs – about $700 million a
year.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:33.700
And where are the benefits of these operations
going to end up? Yep, once again, right back

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.460
in the United States of America.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:39.720
Just like happens in casinos, the house always
wins.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:44.750
So, thanks to Mexico, car companies in the
USA are more competitive and have more profits

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:49.130
while consumers in America can buy much cheaper
cars.

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:54.780
And because of this, Americans have more money
available to go to the cinema, to buy clothes,

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:58.960
to change their smartphones more often, and
take part in all that consumer spending we

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:05.860
so love. All making the economic wheels spin
faster and faster.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:16.880
So, if now it seems that Mexico is important
to the USA, imagine the other way around…

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:21.440
Almost 80% of Mexican exports end up in their
Northern neighbour.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:25.140
So it is no surprise that the threat of the
end of NAFTA has reminded many of the famous

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:31.390
quote attributed to Porfirio Díaz: "Poor
Mexico, so far from God and so near the USA".

00:08:31.390 --> 00:08:35.830
NAFTA has been incredibly positive for Mexico.
As a quick example, do you know which is the

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:41.550
biggest employer in Mexico? Well, that’s
Walmart.

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:46.281
Unfortunately, this huge investment by US
companies hasn’t had the growth that was

00:08:46.281 --> 00:08:51.720
expected in Mexico. Why? Well, for that, we
need to look for responsibility inside the

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:52.720
country.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:56.680
The truth is that Mexican politicians haven’t
know how to take advantage of this fantastic

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:02.410
opportunity to make Mexico more modern and
competitive. But you know, bad politics equals

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:04.050
bad results.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:13.070
However, does this all mean Mexico has to
kneel in front of Donald Trump? Certainly

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:20.510
not…
Donald Trump has threatened Mexico with high

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:25.100
tariffs on their products IF THEY REFUSE TO
PAY FOR THE WALL. To be specific, Trump´s

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:28.340
administration has threatened a 20% tariff
increase…

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:33.300
For starters, well this is illegal. There
are rules in the World Trade Organization

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:37.140
that prevent this from happening. One of these
rules is known as "the most favoured nation",

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:41.010
which means that no member of the WTO can
discriminate against any other.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:45.230
In fact, the maximum tariff would have to
be limited to a 3% in case that NAFTA were

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:50.230
abolished. This would be a tax for consumers
and American companies and wouldn´t affect

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:51.840
trade between the two nations.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:56.610
But even if the US (with the Congress approval)
were willing to ignore international law,

00:09:56.610 --> 00:10:00.210
Mexico has enough resources to fight it and
try to stop this:

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:04.570
First, Mexico would be able to take advantage
of the influence of the multinational corporations

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:08.430
operating in Mexico which could stand to lose
billions of dollars.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:12.420
And this is not just the influence of a few
rich men... We are talking about all these

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:18.590
companies’ workers and their shareholders,
as well as millions of individual savers throughout

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:19.590
the US.

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:24.160
And second: relations between Mexico and the
USA aren't just commercial: immigration, drug-trafficking,

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:29.130
terrorism are just another few aspects of
their relationship... If Trump attacks economically,

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:33.820
Mexico could fight back, for example, by letting
the US drown in drugs and immigration waves

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:36.150
from all countries across Latin America.

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:41.390
The fact is that the USA and Mexico need each
other more than it seems, and Mexico is not

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:46.051
in as weak of a position as Trump thinks.
Although Peña Nieto is unpredictable, it

00:10:46.051 --> 00:10:50.280
does look like he understands this, and the
message from the government in Mexico is clear:

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:55.090
Let's talk about NAFTA, we will renegotiate
this treaty, but then other treaties are going

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:57.080
to be open to negotiation as well.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:04.060
So, what do you think? Should Mexico defend
itself tooth and nail even with the risk of

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:08.440
triggering a trade war with its neighbour?
Or should the nation be patient, let things

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:13.140
go and look for a conciliatory solution? Let
us know in the comments below, we read them

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:16.670
all and try to reply when we can! Don’t
forget we put out brand new videos every Monday

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:20.620
and Thursday, so don’t forget to subscribe!
And as always, I’ll see you in the next

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:37.440
video.

